     'The whole earth in one volume — landforms, climate, oceans, people and the economic world', 'Secondary school geography → university survey + UPSC (Geography optional, GS-I lines)/AP Human Geography readiness', 'The earth’s full survey with the human half in the same volume (the Certificate arc’s own design). The geographic eye first (geography as a discipline, the map’s craft, the grid); the physical core — the earth’s structure and the plate-tectonic engine, the geomorphic processes and the landforms’ census, the atmosphere and the climate’s world with its change honestly priced, the oceans’ register, the soils and the biomes; then the human half — the population and migration’s register, the settlements and the cultural-political landscapes, the economic geography (agriculture, industry, transport and trade, the resources’ distribution); then the Indian spine (the physical environment, the drainage, the population, the resources, the agriculture-industry-transport register); and the synthesis with the doorstep. Cross-references Environmental Studies (the ecological register), Anthropology (the human register) and hands off to the Engineering shelf’s water-resources book for the applied water census and to the reader’s own map sense.',
     ['#059669', '#047857'], 'Geography owns the earth’s whole survey — physical and human in one volume (the backlog’s two folded lines, the Goh Cheng Leong design). Environmental Studies owns the ecological-curriculum register (the pollution and conservation’s human dimension — this book teaches the physical frame, that book the environment-society curriculum); the Engineering shelf’s water-resources-engineering owns the applied water census (dams, irrigation, hydrology’s engineering); the Mathematics shelf owns the projection and survey mathematics (cross-referenced); the UPSC Geography optional and the AP Human Geography register are the two exam spines; Indian History and Sociology cross-reference for the India register’s human content.',
     "- Goh Cheng Leong, Certificate Physical and Human Geography (the FULL OUP India contents read this pass: PART 1 PHYSICAL GEOGRAPHY — The Earth and the Universe; The Earth's Crust; Volcanism and Earthquakes; Weathering, Mass Movement and Erosion; the climatology, oceanography and biogeography chapters; PART 2 WEATHER CLIMATE AND VEGETATION; PART 3 HUMAN GEOGRAPHY — population, settlements and the economic register; the 25-chapter / 7-part structure) — india.oup.com/product/certificate-physical-and-human-geography-9789354975660.\n- NCERT Fundamentals of Physical Geography class 11 (the chapter list read this pass — Geography as a Discipline; The Origin and Evolution of the Earth; Interior of the Earth; Distribution of Oceans and Continents; Geomorphic Processes; Landforms and their Evolution; Composition and Structure of the Atmosphere; Water in the Atmosphere; World Climate and Climate Change; Water Oceans; Movements of Ocean Water — kegy2ps.pdf at ncert.nic.in + the jagranjosh chapter list) + India Physical Environment class 11 (kegy1ps.pdf opened this pass) + Fundamentals of Human Geography class 12 (legy101.pdf — Human Geography: Nature and Scope; The World Population; Human Development; the Primary/Secondary/Tertiary activities; Transport and Communication; International Trade — the hitbullseye chapter list read this pass).\n- The UPSC Geography optional register (read this pass — drishtiias.com/upsc-optional-subjects/geography: Paper I Principles — Geomorphology (factors controlling landform development; endogenetic and exogenetic forces), Climatology, Oceanography, Biogeography, the human-geography perspectives, Economic Geography, Regional Planning; Paper II the Geography of India).\n- AP Human Geography (the seven-unit register read this pass — apstudents.collegeboard.org: Thinking Geographically; Population and Migration; Cultural Patterns; Political Patterns; Agriculture and Rural Land-Use; Cities and Urban Land-Use; Industrial and Economic Development) as the world-exam spine.",
